### What is the priority range?

Every Java thread carries a priority field. You read it with `getPriority()` and change it with `setPriority(int)`. The three constants are `Thread.MIN_PRIORITY` (1), `Thread.NORM_PRIORITY` (5), and `Thread.MAX_PRIORITY` (10). Passing anything outside 1-10 throws `IllegalArgumentException`.

```java
package com.bank.thread;

public class PriorityDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread worker = new Thread(() -> processSettlement(), "settlement-worker");
        worker.setPriority(Thread.MAX_PRIORITY);   // 10
        System.out.println(worker.getPriority());  // 10
        worker.start();
    }

    private static void processSettlement() {
        System.out.println("Processing settlement with priority: "
            + Thread.currentThread().getPriority());
    }
}
```

### How does priority inheritance work?

A new thread inherits the priority of the thread that created it, not always the default of 5. If your main thread has been set to priority 7 and you spawn a worker, that worker starts at 7.

```java
package com.bank.thread;

public class PriorityInheritanceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread.currentThread().setPriority(7);
        Thread child = new Thread(() -> {
            System.out.println("Child priority: " + Thread.currentThread().getPriority());
        });
        child.start();
        child.join();
        // prints 7, not 5: inherited from the creating thread
    }
}
```

### How does Java priority map to the OS?

Java has 10 levels, but Linux uses nice values (-20 to +19), Windows has 7 scheduling priority levels, and macOS has another scheme. The JVM squeezes the Java range into whatever the platform offers. Several Java levels can collapse onto the same OS level. On many Linux JVMs, setting priority has no observable effect without elevated privileges (`CAP_SYS_NICE`).

### What does the JLS guarantee?

The Java Language Specification is explicit: priority is only a hint. It does not say a higher-priority thread will be scheduled first, more often, or at all. Any application logic that depends on scheduling order based on priority is formally incorrect.

### Why do modern schedulers ignore it?

Linux's Completely Fair Scheduler (CFS) allocates CPU proportionally to all runnable threads so no thread is indefinitely denied CPU time. Honouring strict priority would reintroduce starvation and priority inversion, which CFS was designed to prevent.

| Problem | Description | Banking Impact |
|---------|-------------|----------------|
| Priority inversion | High-priority thread waits on a lock held by a low-priority thread that never gets scheduled | Settlement thread waits on audit thread's lock |
| Starvation | Low-priority threads like audit or logging never run because high-priority work keeps arriving | Audit thread never completes, compliance violated |

### What should you use instead?

Use separate thread pools for resource isolation, a `PriorityBlockingQueue` for application-level ordering, or cgroups/container CPU limits for hard CPU guarantees.

```java
package com.bank.pool;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class ResourceIsolation {
    // WRONG: relying on setPriority
    // settlementThread.setPriority(Thread.MAX_PRIORITY);
    // reconThread.setPriority(Thread.MIN_PRIORITY);

    // CORRECT: separate pools with explicit capacity
    private static final ExecutorService settlementPool =
        Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);  // 10 threads dedicated to settlements
    private static final ExecutorService reconPool =
        Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);   // 2 threads for reconciliation
}
```

### How does PriorityBlockingQueue provide application-level ordering?

Instead of relying on OS thread scheduling, you can use a `PriorityBlockingQueue` to ensure high-priority tasks are processed first by the pool's workers.

```java
package com.bank.pool;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class PriorityPoolDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PriorityBlockingQueue<Runnable> queue = new PriorityBlockingQueue<>(100);
        // Workers pull from the queue in priority order
        // This is application-level priority, not OS-level
    }
}
```

### What about virtual threads and priority?

Virtual threads (Java 21) do not support priority at all. The virtual thread scheduler ignores `setPriority()`. This reinforces the point that priority is a legacy mechanism that should not be relied upon.

### What are common mistakes?

The wrong approach uses setPriority for correctness or resource allocation. The correct approach uses dedicated pools, bounded queues, and application-level priority queues.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How does priority inheritance in RTOS systems differ from Java? Real-time operating systems implement priority inheritance: when a low-priority thread holds a lock needed by a high-priority thread, the low-priority thread temporarily inherits the high priority. Java does not have this mechanism.
- Can you change a thread's priority while it is running? Yes, `setPriority()` can be called at any time, but the effect depends on the OS scheduler.
- How do virtual threads handle priority? They ignore it entirely. The virtual thread scheduler uses a work-stealing ForkJoinPool.
